plan ServerRendering
title   Server rendering and hydration
status  proposed
goal
    Render server data and hydrate it without mismatched HTML, duplicate fresh-data fetches,
    replayed mutations, lost client edits or data shared between requests or principals.
outcomes
    O1  Capture a request's data
        flow-state/server exports dehydrate(setup, prepare, { scope, within, maxBytes? }), returning
        Promise<Hydration<A>>. Hydration<A> is opaque, JSON-safe and usable as an RSC prop.
        Each call owns a fresh Runtime, waits for readiness, calls prepare once, settles, captures
        and disposes before resolving. The Runtime is never returned or shared with another call.

        setup is a RuntimeSetup<A> without persistence. Persistence is a type error; untyped use
        rejects before construction. prepare(runtime) is synchronous and returns void; it may
        ensureActor, createActor and send on handles. Returned asynchronous work is rejected,
        never awaited. A throwing prepare or failed readiness, capture or cleanup rejects the
        call; cleanup still runs, and simultaneous work and cleanup failures are both preserved.
        Disposal follows @plan.Runtime.O6. Successful capture with failed cleanup returns no 
        payload.

        scope is required, derived from server authentication, and obeys @plan.Persistence.O1.
        within is a required finite positive Duration.Input. maxBytes defaults to 262,144 and
        accepts integers from 1 through 2,097,152. Invalid options reject before construction.
        Server redirect and notFound decisions call services or operations directly; dehydrate
        does not expose actor state to server components for these decisions.
    O2  Bounded settling
        After prepare, capture waits until all queued events and turns, finite lookups (including
        chained reads and repairs), commits and outcome deliveries finish, or within expires.
        Streams and future schedules do not delay settling. Capture uses one consistent view.
        A chain of dependent lookups settles as a whole, rather than stopping between its reads.

        Expiry returns the current data, reports DehydrateUnsettled, and identifies missing data
        and dropped queued events. Queued events are never transferred or replayed. A send cycle
        cannot keep dehydrate waiting beyond within; cleanup remains subject to @plan.Runtime.O6.
    O3  Transfer safe, confirmed data
        Transfer shared actors and the providers needed for their context, together with data
        read by active activities, including activities of local actors. Local actors themselves
        do not transfer. Persistence flags do not limit hydration selection.
        Transfer actor state and memory, confirmed resource and entity data, loaded pages and
        hasMore, entity staleness, latest stream values and counts, remaining schedule delays and
        pending-commit facts. Recompute context from the transferred providers. Lazy feature
        transfer follows @plan.LazyFeatures.O4.

        Never transfer optimistic values, predictions, queued events, errors, issues, placeholder
        values or running work. A missing, failed or unencodable lookup renders Pending without
        data and retries on client activation. A refetch over existing data transfers that data;
        client freshness determines whether another fetch is needed. An in-flight commit becomes
        unknown with its attempt under @plan.Operations.O3 and reports DehydrateCommitInFlight;
        hydration never reissues it automatically.

        Encode values using their declared Schemas. Omit values without a usable Schema or whose
        encoded form is not JSON-safe or exceeds the bounds of @plan.Inspection.O2; never coerce
        them. List every omitted item and its reason without exposing the omitted value.
        Sensitive entity fields are absent from the client base. Omit a whole resource or stream
        value whose Schema contains sensitive fields. Omit an actor with sensitive memory and
        every consumer dependent on it through context. Readers needing omitted data fetch it.

        maxBytes bounds the complete payload's UTF-8 JSON size. Exceeding it rejects with
        DehydrateTooLarge; no partial payload is returned. A failed consistent capture rejects
        rather than returning a mixture of actor and store versions.
    O4  Matching first render
        flow-state/react exports <Hydrate payload={payload}>{children}</Hydrate>. It requires a
        FlowProvider, otherwise throws HydrateOutsideProvider. It renders children without
        suspending and supports nested boundaries and independently streamed segments.

        During server rendering and browser hydration, Flow hook results depend only on props
        and ancestor Hydrate payloads, nearest boundary first. Sibling segments, live client data
        and elapsed time cannot change this first view. Selectors, context and passive data reads
        use the same view throughout a render. useActorByRef lookup returns null on both sides
        when no ancestor payload contains its actor. Actors absent from these payloads use the
        pre-attach behavior of @plan.React.O2.

        After commit, merge with live data under @O5 before descendant hooks activate their work;
        differing live data causes a subsequent render, never a hydration mismatch. Rendering,
        including a discarded render, starts no work, subscriptions, schedules, persistence writes
        or evidence. Hydrated actors obey admission under @plan.Runtime.O3; rejection applies none
        of the payload, reports HydrationRejected and falls back to ordinary client loading.

        Incompatible app payloads report HydrationIncompatible; malformed payloads, including
        undeclared lazy features or exceeded decode bounds, report HydrationMalformed. Both fall
        back to client loading. Scope and cross-request violations instead throw under @O6.
    O5  Preserve client work when merging
        At commit, an existing client actor wins over its hydrated copy. On first-load restore,
        persisted actors also win, preserving drafts regardless of their contents. The first
        render still shows the server view; the restored draft appears after hydration.
        Context then reflects the winning providers, including providers supplied by hydration.

        For resource data, the younger value wins; equal age keeps the client value. Never replace
        a client entry with an optimistic write pending or a lookup in flight. Hydrated data wins
        over persisted data except where entity versions determine the winner.
        For entity fields, greater versions win and lower versions lose. Equal versions and
        unversioned fields keep present client values; differing values trigger contested-field
        repair under @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Fill absent fields. Existing answers win but become
        stale with cause hydrate when their references differ. New answers are fresh unless the
        client has accepted an entity change, prediction, uncertain mark or queued write since
        readiness; then they are stale with cause hydrate. Existing stream lanes win. Client
        predictions remain applied over any merged confirmed base.

        Activation never replays initial/input processing, context-change events, queued events
        or finite work. An unchanged activity does not redeliver an already-observed value;
        genuinely newer client data can deliver a new value outcome. Changed activity inputs
        reconcile normally. Fresh resource data avoids a duplicate lookup; freshness follows
        @plan.Operations.O2. Age increases by max(0, clientNow - capturedAt), so a client clock
        behind the server cannot make captured data younger.

        Streams open new sessions while displaying their transferred latest values. Schedules
        subtract the same nonnegative elapsed time from their remaining delay; an overdue schedule
        fires once at activation, never in a burst or merely because its baseline was restored.
        Shared actor retention starts at activation. Persistable hydrated actors subsequently
        follow @plan.Persistence.O3.

        Navigation payloads use these same rules on the running client. Reapplying the same
        payload is a no-op; a prefetched payload ages while waiting to render.
    O6  Isolate requests and principals
        Concurrent dehydrate calls share no mutable request state. A server render gets a fresh,
        inert render Runtime; it starts no services, storage or work. Reusing a render Runtime
        across requests throws HydrationCrossRequest before applying the second request's data.
        Segments from one RSC request are accepted together; browser navigation accepts later
        requests without discarding the existing client Runtime.

        A Runtime configured with persistence accepts only its configured scope. Otherwise the
        first accepted payload locks its scope. A mismatch throws HydrationScopeMismatch and
        applies nothing. Changing principal requires a new Runtime under a keyed FlowProvider.
        Matching scope is an isolation check; authentication remains the host's responsibility.
        Server-only import behavior belongs to @plan.Package.O1.
    O7  Streaming and navigation work in real hosts
        Next.js App Router supports an authenticated server setup without persistence, a browser
        FlowProvider, and Hydrate boundaries around server-prepared client subtrees. Layouts and
        independently streamed segments may each capture their own payload. Plain React SSR uses
        the same dehydrate, FlowProvider and Hydrate calls with renderToPipeableStream and
        hydrateRoot. Hosts embedding JSON in scripts must escape it safely for HTML.
        Streaming order, selective hydration, client navigation, router-cache replay and prefetching
        obey @O4 and @O5. Flow hooks never suspend; host Suspense boundaries may stream segments.
verification
    V1  SSR test: nested and sibling boundaries hydrate in any order without HTML mismatch.
    V2  Isolation test: cross-request reuse and wrong scopes throw without applying data.
    V3  Settle Story: chained reads finish; streams and infinite sends cannot prevent timeout.
    V4  Freshness Story: fresh data avoids fetching; missing or aged data fetches despite skew.
    V5  Replay Story: no repeated work or outcomes; overdue schedules fire once; commits stay 
         unknown.
    V6  Merge Story: drafts and predictions survive; conflicts repair; repeated payloads do nothing.
    V7  Redaction test: unsafe values are omitted and reported; sensitive data never transfers.
    V8  Capture tests: invalid inputs reject; all failures dispose and preserve cleanup errors.
    V9  Admission test: rejects apply nothing; discarded renders start no work or writes.
    V10  Next.js test: streaming, navigation, cached replay and draft reload obey @O4 and @O5.
questions
    Q2  Should hydration scope, principal() and persistence.scope share one setup field?
        They currently agree by host convention; a unified field would change configuration.
    Q3  Deferred to build time
        Payload envelope and identity encoding.
        Internal capture and install sharing with persistence.
        Capture-conflict retry strategy.
        Codec implementation and canonical ordering.
        Request identity generation and sequencing.
        Decode caching and discarded-render storage.
        Detailed trace and inspection fields.
        Static warning activation for missing result Schemas.
        Story payload labels and orchestration: @plan.Testing.O7.
        Host export-condition layout: @plan.Package.O1.
